<% content_for :title, "Label" %>
<div class="mb-8">
  <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Forms</p>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">Label</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground">
    A <%= link_to "Field", field_path, class: "text-primary underline" %> renders this for you, with the text
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">form.label</code> would show for the record and a required marker when the model validates presence. On its own
    it is a native <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">&lt;label&gt;</code>, dimmed when the control it names — or the group it sits in — is disabled.
  </p>
</div>

<%# Preview %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Preview</h2>
<div id="label-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background flex flex-wrap items-start gap-6 p-12 mb-8">
  <div class="grid gap-2 w-56">
    <p class="text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">default</p>
    <%= render(Ui::LabelComponent.new(for: "label-email")) { "Email" } %>
    <%= render Ui::InputComponent.new(id: "label-email", name: "email", class: "peer") %>
  </div>
  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 w-56">
    <%= render Ui::InputComponent.new(type: "checkbox", id: "label-terms-disabled", name: "terms_disabled", class: "peer size-4 shrink-0", disabled: true) %>
    <%= render(Ui::LabelComponent.new(for: "label-terms-disabled")) { "Accept the terms" } %>
  </div>
  <div class="grid gap-2 w-56 group" data-disabled="true">
    <p class="text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">dimmed (group disabled)</p>
    <%= render(Ui::LabelComponent.new(for: "label-notes-group")) { "Notes" } %>
    <%= render Ui::InputComponent.new(id: "label-notes-group", name: "notes_group", disabled: true) %>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">
  The second example's checkbox carries <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">class: "peer"</code> and is disabled, so
  the label after it dims automatically — no Ruby keyword on the label itself, and the control has to precede the label in markup for the
  CSS sibling selector to reach it. The third wraps both in a <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">group</code>
  carrying <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">data-disabled="true"</code>, which works whichever order they're in.
</p>

<%# Usage %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Usage</h2>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">ERB</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#label-usage" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="label-usage" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_label_usage) %></code></pre>
</div>

<%# Options %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Options</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border mb-8">
  <table class="w-full text-sm">
    <thead class="bg-muted">
      <tr>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Name</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Type</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Default</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
      <% [
        ["for",    "String", "nil", "The id of the control this label names."],
        ["class",  "String", "nil", "Merged over the default classes with tailwind_merge — the caller always wins."],
        ["…",      "Any",    "—",   "Every other keyword (id:, data:, aria:, …) is forwarded to the root element."],
      ].each do |name, type, default, desc| %>
        <tr class="hover:bg-muted/50">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-primary"><%= name %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground"><%= type %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground"><%= default %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground"><%= desc %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<%# Accessibility %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Accessibility</h2>
<ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground space-y-2 mb-8 list-disc ps-5">
  <li>A real <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">&lt;label for&gt;</code>: clicking it focuses the control, and it is the control's accessible name.</li>
  <li>Dimming is CSS only. <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">peer-disabled:*</code> tracks a sibling control carrying <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">class: "peer"</code>, which
      has to come before the label in markup; <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">group-data-[disabled=true]:*</code> tracks an ancestor with
      <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">class: "group"</code> and <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">data-disabled="true"</code>, in any order.</li>
  <li>A dimmed label is still read: disabled is announced by the control, not by the label's colour.</li>
</ul>

<%# Not this %>
<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">When not to use it</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">
  In a form, use <%= link_to "Field", field_path, class: "text-primary underline" %>, which renders the label, points it at the
  control and keeps the ids in step. A group of radios or checkboxes is named by a legend-like group label, which
  <%= link_to "Choices", choices_path, class: "text-primary underline" %> inside a Field provides — not a label pointing at the first box.
</p>
